⭐ What is AWS Config?
AWS Config is a service that records and tracks the configuration of your AWS resources.

Think of AWS Config like a CCTV camera for your AWS account:

It records who changed what

It keeps history of resource changes

It checks compliance using rules

It helps with auditing & security

⭐ What is a Configuration Recorder?
Configuration Recorder = The engine that records AWS resource changes

👉 Without the recorder, AWS Config will NOT capture anything.

What it Does:
Records configuration of resources (EC2, S3, IAM, etc.)

Tracks changes over time (version history)

Sends configuration snapshots to S3

Sends configuration change notifications to SNS

Simple Analogy:
Think of the recorder as the “CCTV recording button.”
If the recorder is OFF → nothing gets recorded.

How DevOps Engineers Use It:
Track if someone changed a security group rule

Check S3 bucket access changes

Detect deleted resources (e.g., EC2, S3 bucket)

Keep logs for audits (ISO, SOC2, PCI, etc.)

⭐ Simple Real-Time Example for Configuration Recorder
Scenario:
Your team wants to know whenever someone changes a security group.

Steps:
Enable AWS Config

Turn ON configuration recorder

Select resources to record → choose "All resources"

Now suppose:

Developer increases SSH port open to 0.0.0.0/0

AWS Config Recorder will:

Capture this change

Store old and new configuration

Mark the resource as non-compliant (if rule exists)

👉 This helps you review who opened the port and revert it.

⭐ What is AWS Config Aggregator?
Aggregator = Collects AWS Config data from multiple accounts & regions
(It aggregates → collects → merges → combines)

Why it Exists:
Companies with 10–100 AWS accounts need a single dashboard to view:

Config history

Compliance status

Resource changes

Violations

Simple Analogy:
Imagine 10 buildings with separate CCTV systems.
An Aggregator is like a central monitoring room showing all cameras from all buildings.

⭐ How DevOps Engineers Use Aggregator
Centralized compliance view for multiple AWS accounts

Check all S3 buckets across all accounts for public access

Track if any EC2 instance in any region has unencrypted EBS volumes

Useful for Security, Governance, and DevOps teams

